<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-measures-of-dispersion" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Measures of Dispersion</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        3.0 Statistics and Probability
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        3.1 Statistics II
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        3.1 Statistics III
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p component="core">
                        Determine mean, variance and standard deviation using assumed mean for grouped data; Determine interquartile range and quartile deviation for grouped and ungrouped data analytically and graphically
                    </p>
                    <p component="essential">
                        Determine mean, variance and standard deviation for grouped data; Estimate median, quartiles, interquartile range and quartile deviation from cumulative frequency curve
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        3 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Sheet title: Measures of spread/dispersion for ungrouped and grouped data (IQR, quartile deviation, mean, variance, standard deviation)
         Content summary: Range, interquartile range, quartile deviation, variance and standard deviation (Core: using assumed mean); data-collection project.
         Suggested activity: Groups compare the spread of marks in two subjects using the interquartile range and standard deviation, and present their findings.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Two data sets shown as dot plots; sliders shift or spread one set and its mean, variance and standard deviation update live. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (b), (f); Essential (b), (e). Lessons 4.1 to 4.3 as subsections. Lesson 4.3 also carries outcome (g) Core / (f) Essential (value statistics in everyday life), the key inquiry question revisited, the data project and the curriculum links.
         Activity: as planning notes (two subjects' marks compared by IQR and standard deviation, then presented) in lesson 4.3 | changed: lessons 4.1 and 4.2 add their own activities first
         JSXGraph: fig-spread-dot-plots (assets/jsxgraph/spread-dot-plots.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/04-measures-of-dispersion/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 4.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-range-iqr-and-quartile-deviation">
        <title>Range, Interquartile Range and Quartile Deviation</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the range uses the two extreme values; the interquartile range and quartile deviation use the middle half and are not affected by extremes.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the range, interquartile range and quartile deviation of a list, and of grouped data from a cumulative frequency curve.</p></li>
                <li component="core"><p><alert>Do (Core):</alert> calculate the interquartile range and quartile deviation of grouped data.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> compare the consistency of two data sets.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners see that one extreme value changes the range but not the interquartile range.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Start with the question: <q>A commuter must not be late for work. Which route should she take?</q> Let groups argue from the raw times before they calculate.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-two-matatu-routes">
            <title>Two Matatu Routes</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The times, in minutes, of <m>11</m> matatu journeys on each of two routes into Nairobi were recorded.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-two-matatu-routes">
                    <title>Journey Times on Two Routes (minutes)</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row><cell>Route A</cell><cell>25</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>28</cell><cell>45</cell><cell>32</cell><cell>27</cell><cell>35</cell><cell>31</cell><cell>29</cell><cell>26</cell><cell>33</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Route B</cell><cell>22</cell><cell>38</cell><cell>24</cell><cell>36</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>26</cell><cell>34</cell><cell>28</cell><cell>40</cell><cell>25</cell><cell>32</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Put each route's times in order. Find the range of each: the longest time minus the shortest.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>A: <m>25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 35, 45</m>, range <m>20</m>. B: <m>22, 24, 25, 26, 28, 30, 32, 34, 36, 38, 40</m>, range <m>18</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the median and the quartiles of each route.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Positions <m>3</m>, <m>6</m>, <m>9</m>. A: <m>27, 30, 33</m>. B: <m>25, 30, 36</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>For each route find <m>Q_3 - Q_1</m> and half of it.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>A: <m>6</m> and <m>3</m>. B: <m>11</m> and <m>5.5</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>One Route A trip took <m>45</m> minutes because the matatu broke down. Which of your measures does this one trip affect?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Only the range. Without it, A's range would be <m>10</m>; the quartiles hardly move.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Which route is more reliable? Give a reason.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Route A: its middle half of journeys lies within <m>6</m> minutes, against <m>11</m> for B. The range wrongly suggests the routes are alike.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To define the range, interquartile range and quartile deviation and compare them.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (d) shows why the interquartile range is preferred when there are extreme values.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Always order the data first.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For grouped data, the quartiles come from the cumulative frequency curve or the formula of the previous section.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-range-iqr-qd">
            <title>Range, Interquartile Range and Quartile Deviation</title>
            <p>
                The <term>range</term> is the largest value minus the smallest. The <term>interquartile range</term> (IQR) is <m>Q_3 - Q_1</m>, the spread of the middle half of the data, and the <term>quartile deviation</term> (QD, or semi-interquartile range) is half of it, <m>\dfrac{Q_3 - Q_1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The range depends on only the two most extreme values, so one unusual value can change it a lot. The IQR and QD ignore the top and bottom quarters, so they are not affected by extreme values.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-mpesa-iqr">
            <title>M-Pesa Transactions</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The numbers of M-Pesa transactions at an agent in Kisumu on <m>15</m> days were <m>48, 55, 62, 41, 58, 66, 52, 45, 70, 60, 50, 57, 63, 44, 54</m>. Find the range, the interquartile range and the quartile deviation.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>In order: <m>41, 44, 45, 48, 50, 52, 54, 55, 57, 58, 60, 62, 63, 66, 70</m>. The range is <m>70 - 41 = 29</m>.</p>
                <p>With <m>n + 1 = 16</m>, <m>Q_1</m> is the 4th value, <m>48</m>; the median is the 8th, <m>55</m>; <m>Q_3</m> is the 12th, <m>62</m>. So IQR <m>= 62 - 48 = 14</m> and QD <m>= 7</m> transactions.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-milk-iqr-calculation" component="core">
            <title>Milk Sales: IQR by Calculation</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The milk sold, in litres (to the nearest litre), on <m>70</m> days at a dairy kiosk in Nakuru is shown below. Calculate the interquartile range and the quartile deviation.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-milk-sales-core">
                    <title>Milk Sold on 70 Days</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Milk (litres)</cell><cell>10–19</cell><cell>20–29</cell><cell>30–39</cell><cell>40–49</cell><cell>50–59</cell><cell>60–69</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>11</cell><cell>16</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>6</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The cumulative frequencies are <m>5, 16, 32, 52, 64, 70</m>. <m>Q_1</m> is at <m>70 \div 4 = 17.5</m>, in 30–39; <m>Q_3</m> at <m>52.5</m>, in 50–59.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>Q_1 \amp = 29.5 + \frac{17.5 - 16}{16} \times 10 = 30.44\ldots</mrow>
                        <mrow>Q_3 \amp = 49.5 + \frac{52.5 - 52}{12} \times 10 = 49.92\ldots</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Keeping full accuracy, IQR <m>= 49.92 - 30.44 = 19.48 \approx 19.5\,\text{litres}</m> and QD <m>= 9.74 \approx 9.7\,\text{litres}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-milk-iqr-curve">
            <title>Milk Sales: IQR from the Curve</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The milk sold, in litres (to the nearest litre), on <m>70</m> days at a dairy kiosk in Nakuru has frequencies <m>5, 11, 16, 20, 12, 6</m> for the classes 10–19 to 60–69. Use the cumulative frequency curve to estimate the interquartile range and the quartile deviation.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Plot <m>(9.5, 0)</m> and the cumulative frequencies <m>5, 16, 32, 52, 64, 70</m> at the upper class boundaries. Go across from <m>70 \div 4 = 17.5</m> and from <m>52.5</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-milk-ogive">
                    <caption>Reading the quartiles of the milk sales from the curve.</caption>
                    <image width="90%">
                        <shortdescription>Cumulative frequency curve of milk sold on 70 days with dashed lines across from 17.5 and 52.5 to the curve and down to the litres axis at about 30.4 and 49.9 litres.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis milk in litres from 5 to 75; vertical axis cumulative frequency from 0 to 70. Plotted points (9.5, 0), (19.5, 5), (29.5, 16), (39.5, 32), (49.5, 52), (59.5, 64), (69.5, 70) joined by straight line segments. Dashed lines go across from cumulative frequency 17.5 and 52.5 to the graph and down to the litres axis at 30.4 and 49.9.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=12cm, height=8cm, xmin=5, xmax=75, ymin=0, ymax=72,
  xtick={5,15,...,75}, ytick={0,10,...,70}, minor x tick num=4, minor y tick num=4,
  grid=both, major grid style={gray!50}, minor grid style={gray!20},
  xlabel={Milk sold (litres)}, ylabel={Cumulative frequency}]
\addplot[thick, mark=*] coordinates {(9.5,0) (19.5,5) (29.5,16) (39.5,32) (49.5,52) (59.5,64) (69.5,70)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(5,17.5) (30.44,17.5) (30.44,0)};
\addplot[dashed, thick] coordinates {(5,52.5) (49.92,52.5) (49.92,0)};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:5.3,17.5) {17.5};
\node[anchor=south west, font=\small] at (axis cs:5.3,52.5) {52.5};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:30.44,3) {30.4};
\node[anchor=south, font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:49.92,3) {49.9};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p><m>Q_1 \approx 30.4\,\text{litres}</m> and <m>Q_3 \approx 49.9\,\text{litres}</m>, so the IQR is about <m>19.5\,\text{litres}</m> and the quartile deviation about <m>9.7\,\text{litres}</m>.</p>
                <p component="core">These agree with the calculation in <xref ref="example-milk-iqr-calculation"/>: reading a curve drawn with straight segments is a check on the arithmetic.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-two-streams-spread">
            <title>Two Streams</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Two Grade 12 streams, X and Y, each of <m>40</m> learners, sat the same test marked out of <m>50</m>. Their quartiles, read from their cumulative frequency curves, were</p>
                <table xml:id="table-two-streams">
                    <title>Quartiles of Two Streams</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Stream</cell><cell><m>Q_1</m></cell><cell>Median</cell><cell><m>Q_3</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>X</cell><cell>19.3</cell><cell>26.5</cell><cell>34.5</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Y</cell><cell>14.9</cell><cell>25.5</cell><cell>36.1</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>Compare the two streams.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>X: IQR <m>= 34.5 - 19.3 = 15.2</m>, QD <m>= 7.6</m>. Y: IQR <m>= 36.1 - 14.9 = 21.2</m>, QD <m>= 10.6</m>.</p>
                <p>Stream X has a slightly higher median (<m>26.5</m> against <m>25.5</m>) and a smaller interquartile range, so its marks are a little higher on the whole and more consistent. Stream Y has more learners with low marks and more with high marks.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>30.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-range-interquartile-range-and-quartile-deviation">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-range-interquartile-range-and-quartile-deviation" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/04-measures-of-dispersion/Range-interquartile-range-and-quartile-deviation.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-interquartile-range-and-quartile-deviation-by-calculation" component="core">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-interquartile-range-and-quartile-deviation-by-calculation" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/04-measures-of-dispersion/Interquartile-range-and-quartile-deviation-by-calculation.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-range-iqr-qd">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-iqr-seven-values">
                <statement><p>Find the range, the interquartile range and the quartile deviation of <m>9, 3, 14, 5, 12, 1, 10</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Range <m>13</m>; <m>Q_1 = 3</m>, <m>Q_3 = 12</m>; IQR <m>9</m>; QD <m>4.5</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>In order: <m>1, 3, 5, 9, 10, 12, 14</m>. Range <m>14 - 1 = 13</m>. Positions <m>2</m> and <m>6</m> give <m>Q_1 = 3</m> and <m>Q_3 = 12</m>, so IQR <m>= 9</m> and QD <m>= 4.5</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-iqr-rainfall-kisii">
                <statement><p>The daily rainfall, in mm, on <m>11</m> days in Kisii was <m>3.2, 4.5, 2.8, 5.1, 3.9, 4.2, 3.6, 4.8, 3.3, 4.0, 4.6</m>. Find the range, the interquartile range and the quartile deviation.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Range <m>2.3\,\text{mm}</m>; <m>Q_1 = 3.3</m>, <m>Q_3 = 4.6</m>; IQR <m>1.3\,\text{mm}</m>; QD <m>0.65\,\text{mm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>In order: <m>2.8, 3.2, 3.3, 3.6, 3.9, 4.0, 4.2, 4.5, 4.6, 4.8, 5.1</m>. Range <m>5.1 - 2.8 = 2.3</m>. Positions <m>3</m> and <m>9</m>: <m>Q_1 = 3.3</m>, <m>Q_3 = 4.6</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-iqr-goats-calculation" component="core">
                <statement><p>Calculate the interquartile range and the quartile deviation of the goat masses in <xref ref="example-goats-quartiles"/>. Compare with the values read from the curve in <xref ref="example-goats-quartiles-from-curve"/>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>IQR <m>\approx 9.8\,\text{kg}</m>, QD <m>\approx 4.9\,\text{kg}</m>; the same as from the curve.</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>Q_3 - Q_1 = 35.33 - 25.54 = 9.79</m>, so IQR <m>\approx 9.8\,\text{kg}</m> and QD <m>\approx 4.9\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-iqr-tomatoes-calculation" component="core">
                <statement><p>Calculate the interquartile range and the quartile deviation of the tomato masses in <xref ref="ex-quartiles-tomatoes"/>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>IQR <m>\approx 20.2\,\text{g}</m>, QD <m>\approx 10.1\,\text{g}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>88.73 - 68.5 = 20.23</m>, so IQR <m>\approx 20.2\,\text{g}</m> and QD <m>\approx 10.1\,\text{g}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-iqr-seedlings-curve">
                <statement><p>Use your seedling curve from <xref ref="ex-ogive-seedlings"/> to estimate the interquartile range and the quartile deviation.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>IQR <m>\approx 9.1\,\text{cm}</m>, QD <m>\approx 4.5\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-quartile-deviation-reverse">
                <title>Challenge: Working Backwards</title>
                <statement><p>A data set has quartile deviation <m>4</m> and lower quartile <m>23</m>. Find the upper quartile. Every value is then increased by <m>10</m>. What is the new interquartile range?</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Adding the same number to every value moves both quartiles by that number.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>Q_3 = 31</m>; the IQR stays <m>8</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>IQR <m>= 2 \times 4 = 8</m>, so <m>Q_3 = 23 + 8 = 31</m>. After adding <m>10</m>, <m>Q_1 = 33</m> and <m>Q_3 = 41</m>: the IQR is still <m>8</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-range-iqr-qd">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give the activity data already ordered, and a card: range <m>=</m> largest <m>-</m> smallest; IQR <m>= Q_3 - Q_1</m>; QD <m>=</m> IQR <m>\div 2</m>. Assign <xref ref="ex-iqr-seven-values"/> and <xref ref="ex-iqr-rainfall-kisii"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-iqr-seven-values"/>, <xref ref="ex-iqr-rainfall-kisii"/> and <xref ref="ex-iqr-seedlings-curve"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-quartile-deviation-reverse"/>. Extension: make a set of <m>7</m> whole numbers with range <m>30</m> and IQR <m>4</m> (one answer: <m>0, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 30</m>).</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 4.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-variance-and-standard-deviation-grouped">
        <title component="core">Standard Deviation of Grouped Data: the Assumed Mean</title>
        <title component="essential">Standard Deviation of Grouped Data</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> grouped data place every value at its class midpoint, so the mean and standard deviation are estimates.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> estimate the mean, variance and standard deviation of grouped data from <m>\Sigma fx</m> and <m>\Sigma fx^2</m>.</p></li>
                <li component="core"><p><alert>Do (Core):</alert> do the same with an assumed mean, <m>d = x - A</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> find the new mean and standard deviation when every value is changed in the same way.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners estimate the mean and spread of goat masses from a grouped table, using midpoints.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>After part (b), ask: <q>Is 30.4 kg the exact mean mass of these goats?</q> Draw out that we have placed every goat at the middle of its class.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-how-spread-are-the-goats">
            <title>How Spread Are the Goats?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use the masses of the <m>50</m> goats at Garissa market from <xref ref="example-goats-ogive"/>: frequencies <m>3, 7, 12, 14, 9, 5</m> for the classes 15–19 to 40–44 kg.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Write down the midpoint of each class.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>17, 22, 27, 32, 37, 42</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Multiply each midpoint by its frequency and add. Estimate the mean mass.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>fx</m>: <m>51, 154, 324, 448, 333, 210</m>; <m>\Sigma fx = 1520</m>; <m>\bar{x} = 1520 \div 50 = 30.4\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Is this the true mean mass of the <m>50</m> goats? Explain.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>No, it is an estimate: we do not know the actual masses, so each goat is treated as if it weighed the midpoint of its class.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Work out <m>\Sigma fx^2</m> and use <m>\sigma^2 = \dfrac{\Sigma fx^2}{\Sigma f} - \bar{x}^2</m> to estimate the standard deviation.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>fx^2</m>: <m>867, 3388, 8748, 14\,336, 12\,321, 8820</m>; <m>\Sigma fx^2 = 48\,480</m>; <m>\sigma^2 = 969.6 - 924.16 = 45.44</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 6.74\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise the mean and standard deviation of grouped data, and (Core) the assumed-mean method for them.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (c): grouped results are estimates, because every value is placed at its midpoint.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The formulas are those for a frequency table, with <m>x</m> the class midpoint. Divide by <m>\Sigma f</m>, never <m>\Sigma f - 1</m>.</p></li>
                    <li component="core"><p>With an assumed mean, add <m>A</m> back to the mean of <m>d</m>, but never to the standard deviation.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-grouped-variance">
            <title>Grouped Data</title>
            <p>
                For grouped data, let <m>x</m> be the class midpoint. Then
                <md>\bar{x} = \frac{\Sigma fx}{\Sigma f}, \qquad \sigma^2 = \frac{\Sigma fx^2}{\Sigma f} - \bar{x}^2, \qquad \sigma = \sqrt{\sigma^2}</md>.
                These are estimates, because every value is placed at the midpoint of its class. Keep <m>\bar{x}</m> unrounded when you find <m>\sigma^2</m>.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <insight xml:id="insight-grouped-assumed-mean" component="core">
            <title>Grouped Data with an Assumed Mean</title>
            <p>
                Choose <m>A</m>, a midpoint of a class near the middle, and work with <m>d = x - A</m>:
                <md>\bar{x} = A + \frac{\Sigma fd}{\Sigma f}, \qquad \sigma^2 = \frac{\Sigma fd^2}{\Sigma f} - \left(\frac{\Sigma fd}{\Sigma f}\right)^2</md>.
                Subtracting the same number <m>A</m> from every value moves the mean down by <m>A</m> and leaves the spread unchanged, so the standard deviation of <m>x</m> equals that of <m>d</m>.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-goats-standard-deviation">
            <title>Goats: Standard Deviation</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Estimate the mean and standard deviation of the goat masses in <xref ref="act-how-spread-are-the-goats"/>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <table xml:id="table-goats-standard-deviation">
                    <title>Working for the Goat Masses</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Class</cell><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>Midpoint (<m>x</m>)</cell><cell><m>fx</m></cell><cell><m>fx^2</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>15–19</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>17</cell><cell>51</cell><cell>867</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>20–24</cell><cell>7</cell><cell>22</cell><cell>154</cell><cell>3388</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>25–29</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>27</cell><cell>324</cell><cell>8748</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>30–34</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>32</cell><cell>448</cell><cell><m>14\,336</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>35–39</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>37</cell><cell>333</cell><cell><m>12\,321</m></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>40–44</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>42</cell><cell>210</cell><cell>8820</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Total</cell><cell>50</cell><cell></cell><cell>1520</cell><cell><m>48\,480</m></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p><md>\bar{x} = \frac{1520}{50} = 30.4, \qquad \sigma^2 = \frac{48\,480}{50} - 30.4^2 = 969.6 - 924.16 = 45.44, \qquad \sigma = \sqrt{45.44} \approx 6.74</md>
                    The mean mass is about <m>30.4\,\text{kg}</m> and the standard deviation about <m>6.74\,\text{kg}</m>.</p>
                <p component="core">With an assumed mean <m>A = 32</m>: <m>d = -15, -10, -5, 0, 5, 10</m>, <m>\Sigma fd = -80</m> and <m>\Sigma fd^2 = 2400</m>, so <m>\bar{x} = 32 + \dfrac{-80}{50} = 30.4</m> and <m>\sigma^2 = \dfrac{2400}{50} - (-1.6)^2 = 48 - 2.56 = 45.44</m>, the same.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-boda-earnings-core" component="core">
            <title>Boda Boda Earnings</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The daily earnings, <m>e</m> Ksh, of <m>40</m> boda boda riders in Eldoret are shown below. Use an assumed mean of Ksh <m>900</m> to estimate the mean and standard deviation.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-boda-earnings-core">
                    <title>Daily Earnings of 40 Riders</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Earnings (Ksh)</cell><cell><m>400 \le e \lt 600</m></cell><cell><m>600 \le e \lt 800</m></cell><cell><m>800 \le e \lt 1000</m></cell><cell><m>1000 \le e \lt 1200</m></cell><cell><m>1200 \le e \lt 1400</m></cell><cell><m>1400 \le e \lt 1600</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <table xml:id="table-boda-earnings-core-working">
                    <title>Working with <m>A = 900</m></title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Midpoint (<m>x</m>)</cell><cell><m>f</m></cell><cell><m>d = x - A</m></cell><cell><m>fd</m></cell><cell><m>fd^2</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>500</cell><cell>4</cell><cell><m>-400</m></cell><cell><m>-1600</m></cell><cell><m>640\,000</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>700</cell><cell>9</cell><cell><m>-200</m></cell><cell><m>-1800</m></cell><cell><m>360\,000</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>900</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>1100</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>200</cell><cell>1600</cell><cell><m>320\,000</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>1300</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>400</cell><cell>2000</cell><cell><m>800\,000</m></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>1500</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>600</cell><cell>1200</cell><cell><m>720\,000</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Total</cell><cell>40</cell><cell></cell><cell>1400</cell><cell><m>2\,840\,000</m></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p><md>\bar{x} = 900 + \frac{1400}{40} = 935, \qquad \sigma^2 = \frac{2\,840\,000}{40} - 35^2 = 71\,000 - 1225 = 69\,775, \qquad \sigma \approx 264.15</md>
                    The mean daily earnings are about Ksh <m>935</m>, with standard deviation about Ksh <m>264.15</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-boda-earnings-essential" component="essential">
            <title>Boda Boda Earnings</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The daily earnings, <m>e</m> Ksh, of <m>40</m> boda boda riders in Eldoret are shown below. Estimate the mean and standard deviation.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-boda-earnings-essential">
                    <title>Daily Earnings of 40 Riders</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Earnings (Ksh)</cell><cell><m>400 \le e \lt 600</m></cell><cell><m>600 \le e \lt 800</m></cell><cell><m>800 \le e \lt 1000</m></cell><cell><m>1000 \le e \lt 1200</m></cell><cell><m>1200 \le e \lt 1400</m></cell><cell><m>1400 \le e \lt 1600</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>9</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>5</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The midpoints are <m>500, 700, 900, 1100, 1300, 1500</m>. Then <m>\Sigma fx = 37\,400</m> and <m>\Sigma fx^2 = 37\,760\,000</m>, so
                    <md>\bar{x} = \frac{37\,400}{40} = 935, \qquad \sigma^2 = \frac{37\,760\,000}{40} - 935^2 = 944\,000 - 874\,225 = 69\,775, \qquad \sigma \approx 264.15</md>
                    The mean daily earnings are about Ksh <m>935</m>, with standard deviation about Ksh <m>264.15</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <note xml:id="note-step-deviations" component="core">
            <title>Step Deviations (Extension)</title>
            <p>
                When the class widths are equal, the deviations can also be divided by the width <m>c</m>: <m>u = \dfrac{x - A}{c}</m>. For the boda boda earnings, <m>u = \dfrac{x - 900}{200}</m> gives <m>u = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3</m>, <m>\Sigma fu = 7</m> and <m>\Sigma fu^2 = 71</m>. Then <m>\sigma_u^2 = \dfrac{71}{40} - \left(\dfrac{7}{40}\right)^2 = 1.744375</m>, and multiplying back by <m>c</m> gives <m>\sigma = 200 \times \sqrt{1.744375} \approx 264.15</m>, as before. This method is beyond the KICD Grade 12 design.
            </p>
        </note>

        <example xml:id="example-changing-every-value">
            <title>Changing Every Value</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>A thermometer at a school weather station in Nyeri read <m>1.5\,^{\circ}\text{C}</m> too low every day. Its readings had mean <m>24.0\,^{\circ}\text{C}</m> and standard deviation <m>2.5\,^{\circ}\text{C}</m>. Find the mean and standard deviation of the corrected temperatures.</p></li>
                        <li><p>A test was marked out of <m>40</m>; the marks had mean <m>26</m> and standard deviation <m>6</m>. The marks are changed to percentages by multiplying by <m>2.5</m>. Find the new mean and standard deviation.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>(a) Adding <m>1.5</m> to every reading moves the mean up by <m>1.5</m> but does not change the spread: mean <m>25.5\,^{\circ}\text{C}</m>, standard deviation <m>2.5\,^{\circ}\text{C}</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) Multiplying every mark by <m>2.5</m> multiplies the mean and every distance from the mean by <m>2.5</m>: mean <m>65</m>, standard deviation <m>15</m>.</p>
                <p>In general, if every value <m>x</m> becomes <m>ax + b</m> with <m>a \gt 0</m>, the mean becomes <m>a\bar{x} + b</m> and the standard deviation becomes <m>a\sigma</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>30.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-standard-deviation-of-grouped-data">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-standard-deviation-of-grouped-data" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/04-measures-of-dispersion/Standard-deviation-of-grouped-data.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-standard-deviation-of-grouped-data-using-an-assumed-mean" component="core">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-standard-deviation-of-grouped-data-using-an-assumed-mean" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/04-measures-of-dispersion/Standard-deviation-of-grouped-data-using-an-assumed-mean.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-effect-of-scaling-on-the-mean-and-standard-deviation">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-effect-of-scaling-on-the-mean-and-standard-deviation" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/04-measures-of-dispersion/Effect-of-scaling-on-the-mean-and-standard-deviation.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-grouped-standard-deviation">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sd-tea-picked">
                <statement>
                    <p>The mass of tea, <m>m</m> kg, picked in a day by each of <m>40</m> pickers on an estate in Kericho is shown below. Estimate the mean and standard deviation.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Mass (kg)</cell><cell><m>0 \le m \lt 10</m></cell><cell><m>10 \le m \lt 20</m></cell><cell><m>20 \le m \lt 30</m></cell><cell><m>30 \le m \lt 40</m></cell><cell><m>40 \le m \lt 50</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>15</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>3</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
                <answer><p><m>\bar{x} = 24\,\text{kg}</m>, <m>\sigma \approx 10.68\,\text{kg}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Midpoints <m>5, 15, 25, 35, 45</m>; <m>\Sigma fx = 960</m>, <m>\Sigma fx^2 = 27\,600</m>. <m>\bar{x} = 960 \div 40 = 24</m>; <m>\sigma^2 = 690 - 576 = 114</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 10.68\,\text{kg}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sd-heights-assumed-mean" component="core">
                <statement>
                    <p>The heights, in cm (to the nearest cm), of <m>40</m> learners are shown below. Use an assumed mean of <m>162\,\text{cm}</m> to estimate the mean and standard deviation.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Height (cm)</cell><cell>150–154</cell><cell>155–159</cell><cell>160–164</cell><cell>165–169</cell><cell>170–174</cell><cell>175–179</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
                <answer><p><m>\Sigma fd = 35</m>, <m>\Sigma fd^2 = 1375</m>; <m>\bar{x} \approx 162.9\,\text{cm}</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 5.80\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Midpoints <m>152, 157, \ldots, 177</m>; <m>d = -10, -5, 0, 5, 10, 15</m>. <m>\bar{x} = 162 + 35 \div 40 = 162.875</m>; <m>\sigma^2 = 1375 \div 40 - 0.875^2 = 34.375 - 0.765625 = 33.609375</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 5.80\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sd-heights-direct" component="essential">
                <statement>
                    <p>The heights, in cm (to the nearest cm), of <m>40</m> learners are shown below. Estimate the mean and standard deviation.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Height (cm)</cell><cell>150–154</cell><cell>155–159</cell><cell>160–164</cell><cell>165–169</cell><cell>170–174</cell><cell>175–179</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>8</cell><cell>14</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>4</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
                <answer><p><m>\Sigma fx = 6515</m>, <m>\Sigma fx^2 = 1\,062\,475</m>; <m>\bar{x} \approx 162.9\,\text{cm}</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 5.80\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Midpoints <m>152, 157, \ldots, 177</m>. <m>\bar{x} = 6515 \div 40 = 162.875</m>; <m>\sigma^2 = 1\,062\,475 \div 40 - 162.875^2 = 26\,561.875 - 26\,528.265625 = 33.609375</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 5.80\,\text{cm}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sd-seedlings">
                <statement><p>Estimate the mean and standard deviation of the seedling heights in <xref ref="ex-ogive-seedlings"/>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>\bar{x} \approx 24.8\,\text{cm}</m> (<m>24.75</m>); <m>\sigma \approx 6.48\,\text{cm}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Midpoints <m>12, 17, 22, 27, 32, 37</m>; <m>\Sigma fx = 1485</m>, <m>\Sigma fx^2 = 39\,275</m>. <m>\bar{x} = 24.75</m>; <m>\sigma^2 = 654.58\ldots - 612.5625 = 42.02\ldots</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 6.48\,\text{cm}</m>.</p><p component="core">With an assumed mean <m>A = 27</m>: <m>\Sigma fd = -135</m> and <m>\Sigma fd^2 = 2825</m>, giving the same results.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-scaling-marks">
                <statement><p>Quiz marks out of <m>20</m> have mean <m>12.5</m> and standard deviation <m>3.2</m>. Each mark is changed to a percentage (multiplied by <m>5</m>), and then <m>3</m> bonus marks are added. Find the new mean and standard deviation.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Mean <m>65.5</m>; standard deviation <m>16</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Mean <m>5 \times 12.5 + 3 = 65.5</m>. The standard deviation is multiplied by <m>5</m> and not changed by adding: <m>5 \times 3.2 = 16</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sd-from-coded-sums" component="core">
                <statement><p>For <m>20</m> values, an assumed mean <m>A = 50</m> gives <m>\Sigma fd = 30</m> and <m>\Sigma fd^2 = 245</m>. Find the mean, variance and standard deviation.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>\bar{x} = 51.5</m>; <m>\sigma^2 = 10</m>; <m>\sigma \approx 3.16</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>\bar{x} = 50 + 30 \div 20 = 51.5</m>; <m>\sigma^2 = 245 \div 20 - 1.5^2 = 12.25 - 2.25 = 10</m>; <m>\sigma = \sqrt{10} \approx 3.16</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-step-deviations" component="core">
                <title>Step Deviations (Extension)</title>
                <statement><p>Repeat <xref ref="example-boda-earnings-core"/> using <m>u = \dfrac{x - 900}{200}</m>, as in <xref ref="note-step-deviations"/>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>\Sigma fu = 7</m>, <m>\Sigma fu^2 = 71</m>; <m>\sigma \approx</m> Ksh <m>264.15</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>u = -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3</m>; <m>\Sigma fu = -8 - 9 + 0 + 8 + 10 + 6 = 7</m>; <m>\Sigma fu^2 = 16 + 9 + 0 + 8 + 20 + 18 = 71</m>. <m>\bar{x} = 900 + 200 \times \dfrac{7}{40} = 935</m> and <m>\sigma = 200\sqrt{1.775 - 0.030625} \approx 264.15</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-prove-computing-formula">
                <title>Challenge: Two Formulas, One Variance</title>
                <statement><p>Show that <m>\Sigma (x - \bar{x})^2 = \Sigma x^2 - n\bar{x}^2</m>, so the two formulas for the variance always agree.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Expand <m>(x - \bar{x})^2</m> and use <m>\Sigma x = n\bar{x}</m>.</p></hint>
                <answer><p>Expand and use <m>\Sigma x = n\bar{x}</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>\Sigma (x - \bar{x})^2 = \Sigma x^2 - 2\bar{x}\Sigma x + n\bar{x}^2 = \Sigma x^2 - 2n\bar{x}^2 + n\bar{x}^2 = \Sigma x^2 - n\bar{x}^2</m>. Dividing by <m>n</m> gives <m>\dfrac{\Sigma (x - \bar{x})^2}{n} = \dfrac{\Sigma x^2}{n} - \bar{x}^2</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-grouped-standard-deviation">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Provide a table template with columns class, <m>f</m>, <m>x</m>, <m>fx</m>, <m>fx^2</m>. Work through the activity and <xref ref="example-goats-standard-deviation"/> in pairs. Assign <xref ref="ex-sd-tea-picked"/> and <xref ref="ex-scaling-marks"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-sd-tea-picked"/> to <xref ref="ex-scaling-marks"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-prove-computing-formula"/>. Extension: add <m>5\,\text{kg}</m> to every goat mass in the activity, recalculate the mean and standard deviation from the table, and explain the result (answer: mean <m>35.4\,\text{kg}</m>, standard deviation still <m>6.74\,\text{kg}</m>).</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 4.3 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-comparing-data-sets-and-the-project">
        <title>Comparing Data Sets and the Statistics Project</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> to compare data sets, compare a centre and a spread; a smaller spread means more consistent data.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> choose suitable measures (mean and standard deviation, or median and interquartile range) and justify the choice.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> plan and carry out a statistics project on a question from the school or community, and value the use of statistics in everyday decisions.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Groups compare two subjects' marks and present their findings.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Half the groups use the interquartile range and half the standard deviation. Each group presents in two minutes. Close by asking whether the two measures told the same story.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-mathematics-or-kiswahili">
            <title>Mathematics or Kiswahili?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The marks, in per cent, of the same <m>12</m> learners in Mathematics and in Kiswahili are shown below.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-mathematics-or-kiswahili">
                    <title>Marks of 12 Learners (%)</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row><cell>Mathematics</cell><cell>45</cell><cell>62</cell><cell>58</cell><cell>71</cell><cell>52</cell><cell>66</cell><cell>49</cell><cell>75</cell><cell>60</cell><cell>55</cell><cell>68</cell><cell>63</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Kiswahili</cell><cell>58</cell><cell>61</cell><cell>55</cell><cell>64</cell><cell>60</cell><cell>57</cell><cell>63</cell><cell>59</cell><cell>62</cell><cell>56</cell><cell>65</cell><cell>60</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the mean mark in each subject.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Mathematics <m>724 \div 12 \approx 60.3</m>; Kiswahili <m>720 \div 12 = 60.0</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the standard deviation of each subject's marks.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Mathematics <m>\sigma \approx 8.64</m>; Kiswahili <m>\sigma \approx 3.03</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the quartiles and the interquartile range of each.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Positions <m>3.25</m>, <m>6.5</m>, <m>9.75</m>. Mathematics: <m>52.75</m>, <m>61</m>, <m>67.5</m>, IQR <m>14.75</m>. Kiswahili: <m>57.25</m>, <m>60</m>, <m>62.75</m>, IQR <m>5.5</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Present your findings in two or three sentences.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The two subjects have almost the same average, but the Kiswahili marks are far more consistent; both measures of spread say so. Groups should name the measures they used.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <figure xml:id="fig-spread-dot-plots">
            <caption>Drag <q>shift</q>: the Kiswahili mean moves and its standard deviation does not. Drag <q>spread</q>: the standard deviation changes.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-spread-dot-plots" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/spread-dot-plots.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-spread-dot-plots" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>The dot plots show the marks from <xref ref="act-mathematics-or-kiswahili"/>. The mean, variance and standard deviation of each subject are shown on the right.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Drag the <alert>shift</alert> slider. Every Kiswahili mark moves by the same amount. What happens to the mean, and to the standard deviation?</p></li>
                            <li><p>Drag the <alert>spread</alert> slider. Every Kiswahili mark moves away from (or towards) the mean. Find the value of <alert>spread</alert> that makes the two standard deviations about equal.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To set out how to compare data sets and choose measures, and to launch the project.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the groups' presentations compared a centre and a spread; that is the pattern for every comparison.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The mean and standard deviation use every value; the median and interquartile range are not pulled by extreme values.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Return to the key inquiry question of the chapter: where do we use statistics in everyday life? Collect learners' examples, then introduce the project.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-comparing-data-sets">
            <title>Comparing Data Sets</title>
            <p>
                To compare two data sets, compare a measure of centre (mean or median) and a measure of spread (standard deviation or interquartile range), and say what each means in the context. The data set with the smaller spread is the more consistent.
            </p>
            <p>
                The mean and standard deviation use every value, so an extreme value pulls them. The median and interquartile range are not affected by extreme values, so they suit data with a few unusual values, such as incomes. Always say which measures you used and why.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-two-runners">
            <title>Two Runners</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Achieng and Mutua train at a camp in Iten. Their times, in seconds, for <m>400\,\text{m}</m> in eight races were</p>
                <table xml:id="table-two-runners">
                    <title>400 m Times (s)</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row><cell>Achieng</cell><cell>52.4</cell><cell>51.8</cell><cell>53.1</cell><cell>52.0</cell><cell>52.7</cell><cell>51.9</cell><cell>52.5</cell><cell>52.0</cell></row>
                        <row><cell>Mutua</cell><cell>51.2</cell><cell>54.0</cell><cell>50.9</cell><cell>53.6</cell><cell>51.5</cell><cell>52.8</cell><cell>50.6</cell><cell>53.4</cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>Compare the two runners.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Achieng: <m>\Sigma x = 418.4</m>, <m>\bar{x} = 52.3</m> s, <m>\sigma \approx 0.42</m> s. Mutua: <m>\Sigma x = 418.0</m>, <m>\bar{x} = 52.25</m> s, <m>\sigma \approx 1.26</m> s.</p>
                <p>Mutua is very slightly faster on average (by <m>0.05</m> s), but Achieng is far more consistent: her standard deviation is a third of his. A coach choosing a runner who will reliably run about <m>52.3</m> s would choose Achieng.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-two-farms">
            <title>Two Farms</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Over many seasons, Farm P in Trans Nzoia had a median yield of <m>24</m> bags of maize per acre with an interquartile range of <m>6</m> bags. Farm Q had a median of <m>26</m> bags with an interquartile range of <m>13</m> bags. Compare the farms.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Farm Q has the higher typical yield (median <m>26</m> against <m>24</m> bags), but its yields vary much more (IQR <m>13</m> against <m>6</m>), so it has more poor seasons as well as more good ones. A farmer who cannot risk a bad year would prefer Farm P's methods; one who can absorb a poor season might prefer Farm Q's.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <note xml:id="note-spreadsheet-tools">
            <title>Spreadsheet Tools</title>
            <p>
                In a spreadsheet, <c>=AVERAGE(A1:A12)</c>, <c>=MEDIAN(A1:A12)</c> and <c>=STDEV.P(A1:A12)</c> give the mean, median and standard deviation (dividing by <m>n</m>, as in this book). Spreadsheet <c>QUARTILE</c> functions use other position rules, so their quartiles may differ slightly from this book's.
            </p>
        </note>

        <project xml:id="project-statistics-in-our-community">
            <title>Statistics in Our Community</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In groups, use statistics to answer a question about your school or community, and use the answer to make a recommendation.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><p>Choose a question that data can answer, for example: How much water does a household near our school use in a day? How long do learners take to get to school? How do maize prices differ between two markets? How long do Grade 12 learners spend on homework?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Plan how you will collect the data: at least <m>30</m> values, who you will ask or what you will measure, and how you will ask for consent.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Collect and record the data honestly. Do not invent or change values, and do not record names.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Organise the data in a grouped frequency table and draw a cumulative frequency curve.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find the mean and standard deviation, and the median, quartiles and interquartile range.</p></li>
                        <li component="core"><p>Use an assumed mean for the mean and standard deviation, and find deciles or percentiles where they help.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Present your findings to the class in a short talk or poster, with your table, graph and measures.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Make one recommendation based on your data, and say which measures support it.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Before you hand in, check: Is the question clear? Are there at least <m>30</m> values? Is the frequency table correct? Is the curve plotted at the upper class boundaries? Are the measures correct and explained in words? Does the recommendation follow from the data?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </project>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Running the Project</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Timeline</title><p>Two weeks. Day 1: groups of four to five choose and agree their question. Days 2 to 6: collect data outside lessons. Days 7 to 9: organise, draw and calculate. Day 10: presentations.</p></li>
                <li><title>Roles</title><p>Each group names a planner, data collectors, a recorder who checks the raw data, and a presenter. Rotate roles if the project is repeated.</p></li>
                <li><title>Assessment</title><p>Assess against four areas, in our words: the mean and standard deviation; the grouped median and quartiles; drawing and reading the cumulative frequency curve; and interpreting the spread to support the recommendation. For each, decide whether the work exceeds, meets, approaches or is below expectations.</p><p component="core">In the first two areas, look for the assumed-mean method and for deciles or percentiles.</p></li>
                <li><title>Integrity</title><p>Discuss research ethics before data collection: no invented data, consent before asking anyone, and no names or private details in the records.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Curriculum Links</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>Where do we use statistics in everyday life?</p></li>
                <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Digital literacy, through spreadsheets and the interactive graphs; self-efficacy, through planning and running the project.</p></li>
                <li><title>Values</title><p>Integrity, through honest data collection and recording.</p></li>
                <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Life skills: decision making based on data, in the project recommendation.</p></li>
                <li><title>Learning Outcome</title><p>This lesson and the project carry the outcome on valuing the use of statistics in everyday life (outcome (g) in the Core design, (f) in the Essential design).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <p>
            <em>Statistics in everyday life:</em> a matatu Sacco uses the spread of journey times, not only the average, to decide which route to advertise as reliable. What decision in your community could your project data help with?
        </p>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>30.6</c>. Give answers to the accuracy asked for.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-comparing-consistency-using-standard-deviation">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-comparing-consistency-using-standard-deviation" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/15-statistics/04-measures-of-dispersion/Comparing-consistency-using-standard-deviation.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-comparing-data-sets">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-compare-two-classes">
                <statement><p>In a test, class A had mean <m>58</m> and standard deviation <m>6</m>; class B had mean <m>61</m> and standard deviation <m>14</m>. Compare the two classes.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Class B scored higher on average; class A's marks were more consistent.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-which-measures-incomes">
                <statement><p>The monthly incomes of households in a village include one very rich trader. Which average and which measure of spread would you use to describe the incomes, and why?</p></statement>
                <answer><p>The median and the interquartile range: the one very large income would pull the mean and standard deviation up, but not the median or IQR.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-routes-sd-versus-iqr">
                <statement><p>Find the standard deviation of the journey times on each route in <xref ref="act-two-matatu-routes"/>. Compare them with the interquartile ranges.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>A <m>\sigma \approx 5.29</m> min, B <m>\sigma \approx 5.74</m> min. The standard deviations are close because A's <m>45</m>-minute trip inflates its standard deviation; the IQRs (<m>6</m> and <m>11</m>) show that A's middle half is much tighter.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>A: <m>\bar{x} = 341 \div 11 = 31</m>, <m>\Sigma (x - \bar{x})^2 = 308</m>, <m>\sigma^2 = 28</m>, <m>\sigma \approx 5.29</m>. B: <m>\bar{x} = 335 \div 11 = 30.45\ldots</m>, <m>\sigma \approx 5.74</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-recommendation">
                <statement><p>Use <xref ref="example-two-farms"/> to write a two-sentence recommendation for a farmer choosing between the methods of Farm P and Farm Q.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Answers vary; a good answer names a centre and a spread, for example: <q>Farm Q's methods give a higher median yield, but Farm P's yields vary less. If you cannot afford a poor season, use Farm P's methods.</q></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-design-a-survey">
                <title>Challenge: Plan Your Survey</title>
                <statement><p>Plan your project survey: write your question, who or what you will measure and how many, how you will record honestly and ask for consent, and which measures you will use and why.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Answers vary. The teacher checks the plan against the project checklist.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-comparing-data-sets">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a summary table template (centre, spread, what it means) and sentence starters: <q>On average ...</q>, <q>The spread ...</q>. Assign <xref ref="ex-compare-two-classes"/> and <xref ref="ex-which-measures-incomes"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and both examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-compare-two-classes"/> to <xref ref="ex-recommendation"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-design-a-survey"/>. Extension: draw the cumulative frequency curves of the two subjects in the activity on one grid (classes 40–49 to 70–79) and compare them.</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>


        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>
